          "prompt": "Stylized hand-painted 3D game environment art for a fast first-person roguelite shooter, matching the\nreadable visual direction of Gunfire Reborn: bold chunky silhouettes, broad clean faceted planes, two or three dominant\ncolour blocks, soft painted gradients and a few restrained edge accents. East-Asian underground forge-palace fantasy of Zhurong, the Chinese god of fire: massive black iron (xuan tie) with heavy rivets, dark charcoal basalt blocks, deep vermilion lacquer, warm aged bronze ding cauldrons, chunky thick iron chains always attached flat to the surfaces, soot-darkened edges, and a few channels and cracks of molten lava. Lava, embers and fire are painted as opaque flat bright orange-yellow surfaces, never as smoke, loose sparks or transparent effects. Matte stone,\nwood and cloth, restrained highlights on metal, sparse painted wear. No photorealism, micro-grain, dense tiny cracks,\nelaborate miniature carvings, harsh specular highlights or baked cast shadows. Even neutral lighting.\n\nAsset (4m wide x 3m tall x 4m deep): A square raised forge-hall terrace module 4m wide, 3m tall, 4m deep for tiling into large terraces, a ceremonial variant of the podium block with EXACTLY the same outer silhouette: a completely solid block with a thick dark charcoal basalt plinth and cap course, each side face holding one big recessed deep vermilion lacquer panel framed by black iron with big round rivets and a single large aged bronze fire-sun (flame disc) emblem in the middle of the panel, aged bronze corner caps at the top and a flat paved top of dark grey flagstones. NO lava and NO glow. All four sides finished identically so modules tile edge to edge.\n\nOutput: one orthographic model turnaround sheet for 3D reconstruction. Exactly four separate complete views\nof the SAME object in one row, left to right:\n1. FRONT view: the object's front faces the viewer.\n2. LEFT side view: the object turned 90 degrees so its front points to the LEFT edge of the image (camera at the\n   object's right-hand side).\n3. BACK view: the object's back faces the viewer.\n4. RIGHT side view: the object turned 90 degrees so its front points to the RIGHT edge of the image.\nSame scale and same ground line in all four, each centred in its quarter with generous empty margin, never touching or\noverlapping; the whole object fits inside its quarter, nothing cropped.\nPlain flat light neutral grey background (#D6D6D6). No floor, no ground shadow, no text, no labels, no frames, no\nperspective.",
